\u00b2 = 0\n\nFactor n\u00b2:\n\nn\u00b2(1 - 14/k) + mn + m\u00b2 = 0\n\nHmm, this seems a bit complicated. Maybe there's another way to approach this.\n\nSince q is a rational number, 1 + q + q\u00b2 must be a rational number as well. Therefore, 14 divided by this value must be an integer. So, 1 + q + q\u00b2 must be a divisor of 14. But 1 + q + q\u00b2 is a rational number, so to make 14/(1 + q + q\u00b2) an integer, the denominator must divide 14 in the rationals. Since q is a rational number, let's let q = m/n where m and n are coprime positive integers with m < n.\n\nThen, 1 + q + q\u00b2 = 1 + m/n + m\u00b2/n\u00b2 = (n\u00b2 + mn + m\u00b2)/n\u00b2\n\nTherefore, the expression becomes 14 / [(n\u00b2 + mn + m\u00b2)/n\u00b2] = 14n\u00b2/(n\u00b2 + mn + m\u00b2)\n\nThis must be an integer. Let's denote k = 14n\u00b2/(n\u00b2 + mn + m\u00b2), where k is a positive integer.\n\nSo, 14n\u00b2 = k(n\u00b2 + mn + m\u00b2)\n\nRearranged:\n\n14n\u00b2 = kn\u00b2 + kmn + km\u00b2\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n(14 - k)n\u00b2 - kmn - km\u00b2 = 0\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of n. But since n is an integer, perhaps we can look for integer solutions m and n with m < n, gcd(m, n) = 1, and k being a positive integer less than 14 (since k must divide 14n\u00b2, but we need to check possible values).\n\nAlternatively, maybe think of possible values of k. Since k must be a positive integer divisor of 14? Wait, 14/(1 + q + q\u00b2) is k, so k must divide 14? Not necessarily. Because 1 + q + q\u00b2 is a rational number, so k = 14/(1 + q + q\u00b2) must be such that 1 + q + q\u00b2 = 14/k. So, 14/k must be equal to 1 + q + q\u00b2. Since q is a positive rational number less than 1, 1 + q + q\u00b2 must be less than 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, but since q is less than 1, the maximum value of 1 + q + q\u00b2 would be when q approaches 1, so approaching 3. Therefore, 14/k < 3 \u21d2 k > 14/3 \u2248 4.666\u2026, so k must be at least 5. But since k is a positive integer less tha