 e=13\n\nSo the sums are 6,10,8,12,9,13. These correspond to the required missing elements in B: {6,8,9,10,12,13}. Perfect, all are present. So this works.\n\nCase 2: d=6, e=8.\n\nCompute the sums:\n\n1 + d=7\n\n1 + e=9\n\n3 + d=9\n\n3 + e=11\n\n4 + d=10\n\n4 + e=12\n\nSo sums are 7,9,9,11,10,12. But in B, we need {6,8,9,10,12,13}. Here, we have duplicates (9 appears twice) and 11 and 7 instead of 6 and 13. But 7 is already in B, but we need 6 and 13. So this case doesn't work because 6 and 13 are missing, and we have extra 11 and duplicate 9. Thus, Case 2 is invalid.\n\nTherefore, the only valid case is Case 1: d=5, e=9.\n\nTherefore, the set A is {1,3,4,5,9}.\n\nLet me verify all sums:\n\n1+3=4 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n1+4=5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n1+5=6 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n1+9=10 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n3+4=7 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n3+5=8 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n3+9=12 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n4+5=9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n4+9=13 \u2714\ufe0f\n\n5+9=14 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nAll these sums are exactly the elements of B: {4,5,6,7,8,9,10,12,13,14}. Perfect. So the solution is A={1,3,4,5,9}.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there's another possible arrangement. Suppose the middle element c is not 4. Wait, earlier we concluded that since the total sum of A is 22, and a + b + d + e=18, so c=4. That seems solid. So c must be 4.\n\nBut just to be thorough, is there any other way? Suppose the elements are not ordered a < b < c < d < e. Wait, but the problem says \"the set A\", which is a set of elements, so order doesn't matter, but when we talk about two-element subsets, the sums are just combinations. However, to solve the problem, we can order the elements for simplicity. But since the problem doesn't specify order, the actual set is just the collection of these elements. So {1,3,4,5,9} is the answer. Let me confirm once again.\n\nYes, all the sums check out. Therefore, the set A is {1,3,4,5,9}.", "deepseek_solution": "The set \\( A \\) is determined by analyzing the given sums of its two-element subsets. \n\n1. **Identify the smallest and