e \\( A \\leq B \\), we have:\n     \\[\n     A \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Case 2: Radius Intersecting Sector of Area \\( B \\):**\n   - The radius divides \\( B \\) into two parts: \\( y \\) and \\( B - y \\).\n   - Thus, the smallest sector's area can be:\n     \\[\n     \\min(A, y, B - y)\n     \\]\n     which simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     \\min \\left( A, \\frac{B}{2} \\right)\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( B = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - A \\):\n     \\[\n     \\min \\left( A, \\frac{\\pi/2 - A}{2} \\right)\n     \\]\n   - This implies:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\pi/4 - A/2} \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Maximizing Area of the Smallest Sector:**\n   - To maximize \\(\\min \\left( A, \\frac{\\pi/2 - A}{2} \\right)\\), we should consider:\n     \\[\n     A \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n     \\]\n   - If \\( A > \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\pi/4 - A/2} \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n     \\]\n   - This happens exactly when:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Calculating the Final Expression:**\n   - The largest possible area of the smallest sector is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n     \\]\n\nConclusion:\n- Convert \\( \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\) to the form \\( \\frac{a}{b}\\pi \\) where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are relatively prime.\n- Here \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 6 \\).\n\nThus:\n\\[\n100a + b = 100 \\cdot 1 + 6 = 106\n\\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{106}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nGiven four real numbers \\(a, b, c, d\\) that satisfy the following two relations: \\(a+b=c+d\\) and \\(a^{3}+b^{3}=c^{3}+d^{3}\\):\n\na) Prove that \\(a^{5}+b^{5}=c^{5}+d^{5}\\).\n\nb) Can it be concluded that \\(a^{4}+b^{4}=c^{4}+d^{4}\\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to solve part (a) and part (b) of this problem. Let me start by understanding what's given and what's asked.\n\nWe have four real numbers a, b, c, d. They satisfy two equations: a + b = c + d and a\u00b3 + b\u00b3 = c\