ame line (the bisector), that would violate the no three colinear condition.\n\nTherefore, for any pair of points Q and R, there are at most two centers P such that Q and R lie on the circle centered at P. Because those centers must lie on the perpendicular bisector of QR, and there can be at most two such points in S on that bisector.\n\nTherefore, each unordered pair (Q, R) can be counted at most twice in the total count of ordered pairs (P, Q) where Q is on the circle around P. Therefore, the total number of ordered pairs (P, Q) is at most 2 * C(n, 2) = n(n - 1). But we also have that the total number of ordered pairs is at least n*k. So putting these together:\n\nn*k \u2264 n(n - 1)\n\nDivide both sides by n:\n\nk \u2264 n - 1\n\nBut that's a much weaker bound than what we need. The problem states that k < 1/2 + sqrt(2n). So clearly, this approach is too crude. It gives k \u2264 n - 1, which is trivial since k can't be more than n - 1 anyway (since a point can't have more than n - 1 points equidistant from it). But the problem wants a much tighter bound. So this suggests that we need a more refined counting argument.\n\nWait, maybe we need to consider not just that each pair is counted at most twice, but also how many times a single point can be on different circles. Let me think again.\n\nEach point Q is on t_Q circles centered at other points. Then the total number of incidences is the sum over Q of t_Q, which is at least n*k. But each t_Q is the number of circles (centered at some P) that contain Q. Each such circle corresponds to a center P such that Q is on the circle around P with at least k points. So for each Q, how many centers P have Q on their circle?\n\nBut for each P, Q can be on at most one circle around P (since each P has one circle with at least k points, but maybe multiple circles? Wait, no. Wait, the problem states \"for any point P of S there are at least k points of S equidistant from P\". Does this mean that there's a single circle around P