(P\\) (i.e., lie on a circle centered at \\(P\\)). For each \\(P\\), there are at least \\(k\\) points on its circle, forming \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\) pairs. Thus, the total number of such triples is at least:\n\\[\nn \\cdot \\binom{k}{2} = n \\cdot \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Bounding Pairs of Points**  \nFor any pair \\(Q, R\\), the centers \\(P\\) equidistant to both must lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(QR\\). Since no three points are collinear, this bisector contains at most two points of \\(S\\). Hence, each unordered pair \\((Q, R)\\) contributes to at most two triples. The total number of triples is thus bounded by:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\binom{n}{2} = n(n-1).\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Combining the Bounds**  \nEquating the lower and upper bounds for triples:\n\\[\n\\frac{nk(k-1)}{2} \\leq n(n-1).\n\\]\nSimplifying:\n\\[\nk(k-1) \\leq 2(n-1).\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Solving the Quadratic Inequality**  \nRewriting the inequality:\n\\[\nk^2 - k - 2n + 2 \\leq 0.\n\\]\nSolving the quadratic equation \\(k^2 - k - 2n + 2 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula:\n\\[\nk = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 8n - 8}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{8n - 7}}{2}.\n\\]\nTaking the positive root (since \\(k > 0\\)):\n\\[\nk \\leq \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{8n - 7}}{2}.\n\\]\n\n**Step 5: Comparing to the Required Bound**  \nWe need to show:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{8n - 7}}{2} < \\frac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{2n}.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{8n - 7}}{2} < \\sqrt{2n}.\n\\]\nMultiplying by 2 and squaring:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{8n - 7} < 2\\sqrt{2n} \\implies 8n - 7 < 8n - 4\\sqrt{2n} + 1.\n\\]\nSimplifying:\n\\[\n-8 < -4\\sqrt{2n} \\implies \\sqrt{2n} > 2,\n\\]\nwhich holds for \\(n \\geq 2\\). Thus, the inequality \\(k < \\frac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{2n}\\) is proven.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nUnder the given conditions, the maximum number \\(k\\) of equidistant points from any \\(P \\in S\\) must satisfy \\(k < \\frac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{2n}\\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{k < \\frac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{2n}}\\)", "gro