erms of tan(alpha/2):\n\nUsing the identity sin\u03b1 = 2 tan(alpha/2)/(1 + tan^2(alpha/2)), and cos\u03b1 = (1 - tan^2(alpha/2))/(1 + tan^2(alpha/2))\n\nThen:\n\n(1 + sin\u03b1)/cos\u03b1 = [1 + 2 tan(alpha/2)/(1 + tan^2(alpha/2)) ] / [ (1 - tan^2(alpha/2))/(1 + tan^2(alpha/2)) ]\n\n= [ (1 + tan^2(alpha/2)) + 2 tan(alpha/2) ] / (1 - tan^2(alpha/2))\n\n= [ (1 + tan(alpha/2))^2 ] / (1 - tan^2(alpha/2))\n\n= [ (1 + tan(alpha/2))^2 ] / [ (1 - tan(alpha/2))(1 + tan(alpha/2)) ]\n\n= (1 + tan(alpha/2)) / (1 - tan(alpha/2))\n\n= [1 + tan(alpha/2)] / [1 - tan(alpha/2)] = tan(pi/4 + alpha/2)\n\nYes, that's an identity. Therefore, tan(theta2) = tan(pi/4 + alpha/2)\n\nSimilarly, tan(theta1) = cot(alpha/2) = tan(pi/2 - alpha/2)\n\nTherefore, theta1 = pi/2 - alpha/2, and theta2 = pi/4 + alpha/2\n\nWait, if tan(theta1) = cot(alpha/2), then theta1 = pi/2 - alpha/2. Similarly, tan(theta2) = tan(pi/4 + alpha/2), so theta2 = pi/4 + alpha/2.\n\nTherefore, theta1 = 90\u00b0 - alpha/2, theta2 = 45\u00b0 + alpha/2\n\nGiven that theta1 and theta2 are in ratio 13:17, so theta1/theta2 = 13/17.\n\nThus,\n\n(90\u00b0 - alpha/2) / (45\u00b0 + alpha/2) = 13/17\n\nLet\u2019s solve for alpha.\n\nLet\u2019s denote alpha in degrees for clarity. Let\u2019s set x = alpha.\n\nSo:\n\n(90 - x/2) / (45 + x/2) = 13/17\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n17(90 - x/2) = 13(45 + x/2)\n\nCompute left side: 17*90 - 17*(x/2) = 1530 - (17x)/2\n\nRight side: 13*45 + 13*(x/2) = 585 + (13x)/2\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n1530 - (17x)/2 - 585 - (13x)/2 = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n(1530 - 585) + (-17x/2 -13x/2) = 0\n\n945 - (30x)/2 = 0\n\n945 - 15x = 0\n\n15x = 945\n\nx = 945 / 15 = 63 degrees\n\nTherefore, alpha = 63\u00b0, beta = 90\u00b0 - 63\u00b0 = 27\u00b0\n\nWait, but let me verify. If alpha is 63\u00b0, then theta1 = 90\u00b0 - 63\u00b0/2 = 90\u00b0 - 31.5\u00b0 = 58.5\u00b0\n\ntheta2 = 45\u00b0 + 63\u00b0/2 = 45\u00b0 + 31.5\u00b0 = 76.5\u00b0\n\nThen the ratio theta1:theta2 = 58.5:76.5 = divide both by 4.5: 13:17. Exactly. 