l become even unless the GCD was at least 2. So maybe with GCD 1, the sequences must eventually reach a state with at least one odd number. But I don't see how this directly helps.\n\nAlternatively, think about the maximum value in the triple. Suppose M_n = max(a_n, b_n, c_n). We can try to show that M_n is non-increasing and eventually strictly decreases until it reaches a fixed point.\n\nIn the example (3,5,7), M\u2081=7, M\u2082=4, M\u2083=2, M\u2084=2. So it decreased until it stabilized. In another example, (4,6,9), M\u2081=9, M\u2082=5, M\u2083=3, M\u2084=2, M\u2085=1, then stabilized. So it seems that the maximum does tend to decrease over time. Let's see if this is always the case.\n\nSuppose we have a triple (a, b, c) with maximum M. Then in the next step, each term is the absolute difference of the other two. So the new terms are |b - c|, |c - a|, |a - b|. Let's see what the maximum of these can be.\n\nSuppose that M = a, and a \u2265 b, a \u2265 c. Then |b - c| \u2264 a (since b and c are both \u2264 a). Similarly, |c - a| = a - c \u2264 a (since c \u2265 0). |a - b| = a - b \u2264 a. So the new maximum is at most a. But can it be equal to a?\n\nFor |c - a| = a - c. If c < a, then a - c \u2264 a. But if c = 0, then |c - a| = a. So if one of the terms is 0, then |c - a| could be equal to a. For example, if the triple is (a, b, 0), then the next terms are |b - 0| = b, |0 - a| = a, |a - b|. So if a > b, then the next triple is (b, a, a - b). The maximum here is a, which is the same as before. So in this case, the maximum doesn't decrease. However, in the next step, let's see:\n\nIf we have (b, a, a - b). Suppose a > b. Then the next terms are |a - (a - b)| = |b| = b, |(a - b) - b| = |a - 2b|, |b - a| = a - b. The maximum of these is max(b, |a - 2b|, a - b). Depending on the relation between a and b, this maximum might be less than a. For example, if a = 5, b = 3, then next terms are 3, |5 - 6| = 1, 2. Maximum is 3, which is less than 5. \n\nWait, in the prev