er even-sized cities.**\n- If all cities have even sizes, the minimal number of compatriot games occurs when players are distributed as evenly as possible:\n  - **Example:** Five cities with 2 players each yield \\( 5 \\times \\binom{2}{2} = 5 \\) compatriot games.\n  - Larger even-sized partitions (e.g., 6 and 4 players) yield \\( \\binom{6}{2} + \\binom{4}{2} = 15 + 6 = 21 \\) games.\n  - The next feasible partition (8 and 2 players) gives \\( \\binom{8}{2} + \\binom{2}{2} = 28 + 1 = 29 \\) games.\n- **Key observation:** The minimal number of compatriot games for even-sized cities that meets or exceeds 23 is **29** (8+2 partition), but the problem allows for \u226523 games. This is a contradiction because 23 cannot be achieved with even-sized cities.\n\n**Step 4: Conclude cities must have odd sizes.**\n- Since 23 compatriot games cannot be formed with even-sized cities, there must be **at least one city with an odd number of players**.\n- **Consequence:** In any perfect matching (round), an odd-sized city cannot have all its players matched externally. At least one pair must be within the city, ensuring at least one compatriot game per round.\n\n**Final Conclusion:**\nAssuming a round with no compatriot games leads to a contradiction. Therefore, **every round must contain at least one game between compatriots**.\n\n$\\boxed{\\text{In every round, there was at least one game between compatriots.}}$", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial Setup and Understanding the Problem:**\n   - There are 10 participants in the chess tournament.\n   - Each participant plays against every other participant exactly once.\n   - Each participant plays 9 games in total.\n   - At least half of these games (i.e., at least 5 games) for each participant involve players from the same city (referred to as 'compatriots').\n\n2. **Counting Necessary Games:**\n   - For any participant, suppose there is at least one participant who plays against at least 5 compatriots. Therefore, this parti