one or zero.\n\nTherefore, to maximize the number of components, you should place each strip such that it splits an existing component into two. Therefore, each strip can contribute to increasing the count by one. Hence, starting from one component, after n strips, maximum components is 1 + n. Hence, the upper bound is n +1.\n\nBut wait, in the earlier 2x2 example with n=2 strips, the maximum regions were 2, which is equal to n, not n +1. But according to this, it should be at most 3. But maybe in small boards, the bound is not tight. The problem states \"the chessboard can have at most n +1 regions\", so regardless of the size. So even if the board is large enough, you can't have more than n +1 regions. So in small boards, the number might be less, but the upper bound holds.\n\nTherefore, the general proof would involve showing that each strip can increase the number of connected components by at most one. Therefore, by induction, the maximum number after n strips is n +1.\n\nTo formalize this, we can use induction on n.\n\nBase case: n=0. The entire board is one connected region. So 0 +1=1. Correct.\n\nInductive step: Assume that after k strips, the number of regions is at most k +1. Now add the (k +1)-th strip. This strip is placed on the board, covering some squares. These squares were previously part of some regions. Since the strip is a 1xk rectangle, it can overlap with at most one existing region. Because if it were overlapping with multiple regions, those regions must be adjacent, but since regions are disconnected, the strip cannot be placed across two disconnected regions (as they don't share an edge). Therefore, the strip is placed within a single region. By removing this strip (i.e., covering it), we split that region into at most two new regions. Hence, the number of regions increases by at most one. Therefore, the total number of regions becomes at most (k +1) +1= k +2. Hence, by induction, the maximum number of regions after n strips is n +1.\n\nWait