=0. So either S=0 or S^2 -3P +3=0. If S\u22600, then S^2 -3P +3=0. Let me use equation 2: S^2 -2P=10. So we have two equations:\n\n1. S^2 - 3P +3 =0\n\n2. S^2 - 2P =10\n\nSubtract the first equation from the second:\n\n(S^2 - 2P) - (S^2 -3P +3) = 10 -0\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n(-2P) - (-3P +3) =10\n\n=> (-2P +3P -3)=10\n\n=> P -3=10\n\n=> P=13\n\nThen plug back into equation 2: S^2 -2*13=10 => S^2 -26=10 => S^2=36 => S=6 or S=-6.\n\nSo if S=6, then from equation 1: Q=S +2=8.\n\nIf S=-6, then Q= -6 +2= -4.\n\nSo now let's check these possibilities.\n\nCase 1: S=6, P=13, Q=8.\n\nThen the cubic equation would be x^3 -6x^2 +13x -8=0.\n\nDoes this equation have three real roots? Let's check its discriminant.\n\nThe discriminant D of a cubic equation ax^3 +bx^2 +cx +d=0 is given by D=18abcd -4b^3d +b^2c^2 -4ac^3 -27a^2d^2.\n\nFor equation x^3 -6x^2 +13x -8=0, a=1, b=-6, c=13, d=-8.\n\nCompute D:\n\n18*1*(-6)*13*(-8) -4*(-6)^3*(-8) + (-6)^2*(13)^2 -4*1*(13)^3 -27*(1)^2*(-8)^2.\n\nFirst term: 18*1*(-6)*13*(-8) = 18*6*13*8 = 18*624=11232.\n\nSecond term: -4*(-6)^3*(-8) = -4*(-216)*(-8)= -4*1728= -6912.\n\nThird term: (-6)^2*(13)^2=36*169=6084.\n\nFourth term: -4*1*(13)^3= -4*2197= -8788.\n\nFifth term: -27*1*64= -1728.\n\nSum all terms:\n\n11232 -6912 +6084 -8788 -1728.\n\nCalculate step by step:\n\n11232 -6912 = 4320\n\n4320 +6084=10404\n\n10404 -8788=1616\n\n1616 -1728= -112.\n\nSo D=-112. Since the discriminant is negative, the cubic equation has one real root and two complex conjugate roots. But the problem states that the equation has three real roots. Therefore, this case is invalid.\n\nCase 2: S=-6, P=13, Q=-4.\n\nThen the cubic equation is x^3 +6x^2 +13x +4=0.\n\nCheck discriminant again. a=1, b=6, c=13, d=4.\n\nD=18*1*6*13*4 -4*6^3*4 +6^2*13^2 -4*1*13^3 -27*1^2*4^2.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 18*1*6*13*4 = 18*6*52=18*312=5616.\n\nSecond term: -4*216*4= -4*864= -3456.\n\nThird term: 36*169=6084.\n\nFourth term: -4*2197= -8788.\n\nFifth term: -27*16= -432.\n\nTotal D=5616 -3456 +6084 -8788 -432.\n\nCalculate step by step:\n\n5616 -3456=2160\n\n2160 +6084=8244\n\n8244 -8788= -544\n\n-544 -432= -976.\n\nAgain, D is negative, so only one real root. Therefore, this case is also invalid.\n\nThus, the only valid case is S=0, P=-5, Q=2. Which gives the cubic equation x^3 -0x^2 -5x -2= x^3 -5x -2=0.\n\nBut let me verify all the given conditions with this equation to be sure.\n\n1. Product of roots is Q=2, sum of roots is S=0. So product is 2, sum is 0. Then 2=0 +2, which satisfies the first condition.\n\n2. Sum of squares: S^2 -2P =0 -2*(-5)=10. Which matches the second condition.\n\n3. Sum of cubes: S^3 -3SP +3Q=0 -0 +6=6. Which matches the third condition.\n\nAdditionally, as we checked before, the discriminant of x^3 -5x -2. Let's compute it for confirmation. a=1, b=0, c=-5, d=-2.\n\nD=18*1*0*(-5)*(-2) -4*0^3*(-2) +0^2*(-5)^2 -4*1*(-5)^3 -27*1^2*(-2)^2.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 18*0*...=0\n\nSecond term: -4*0*...=0\n\nThird term:0*25=0\n\nFourth term: -4*(-125)=500\n\nFifth term: -27*4= -108\n\nSo total D=0 +0 +0 +500 -108=392.\n\nSince D is positive (392>0), the cubic has three distinct real roots. Wait, but earlier when I analyzed the graph, I thought it had three real roots. Wait, but according to the discriminant, it's positive, so three distinct real roots. Therefore, this equation satisfies all the given conditions.\n\nTherefore, the cubic equation is x^3 -5x -2=0.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me check if there could be another solution where S^2 -3P +3=0. But we saw that for both S=6 and S=-6, the discriminants were negative, so those cubics don't have three real roots, so they are invalid. So the only possible solution is the one with S=0.\n\nTherefore, the final equation is x\u00b3 -5x -2 =0.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe cubic equation is \\boxed{x^3 - 5x - 2 = 0}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given a cubic equation with three real roots, we need to determine the equation using the following conditions:\n1. The product of the roots is 2 more than their sum.\n2. The sum of their squares is 10.\n3. The sum of their cubes is 6.\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas for a cubic equation \\(x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) with roots \\(r_1, r_2, r_3\\):\n- Sum of roots: \\(r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -a\\)\n- Sum of products of roots taken two at a time: \\(r_1r_2 + r_1r_3 + r_2r_3 = b\\)\n- Product of roots: \\(r_1r_2r_3 = -c\\)\n\nLet:\n- \\(S = r_1 + r_2 + r_3\\)\n- \\(P = r_1r_2 + r_1r_3 + r_2r_3\\)\n- \\(Q = r_1r_2r_3\\)\n\nFrom the conditions:\n1. \\(Q = S + 2\\)\n2. \\(r_1^2 + r_2^2 + r_3^2 = 10\\) which translates to \\(S^2 - 2P = 10\\)\n3. \\(r_1^3 + r_2^3 + r_3^3 = 6\\) which translates to \\(S^3 - 3SP + 3Q = 6\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(Q = S + 2\\) into the third condition:\n\\[S^3 - 3SP + 3(S + 2) = 6\\]\nSimplifying:\n\\[S^3 - 3SP + 3S + 6 = 6\\]\n\\[S(S^2 - 3P + 3) = 0\\]\n\nThis gives two cases:\n1. \\(S = 0\\)\n2. \\(S^2 - 3P + 3 = 0\\)\n\nFor \\(S = 0\\):\n- From \\(S^2 - 2P = 10\\), we get \\(0 - 2P = 10 \\implies P = -5\\)\n- From \\(Q = S + 2\\), we get \\(Q = 2\\)\n\nFor \\(S^2 - 3P + 3 = 0\\) and \\(S^2 - 2P = 10\\):\n- Solving these, we find \\(P = 13\\) and \\(S = \\pm 6\\), leading to equations with negative discriminants (invalid as they don't have three real roots).\n\nChecking the cubic equation for \\(S = 0\\), \\(P = -5\\), \\(Q = 2\\):\n- The equation is \\(x^3 - 5x - 2 = 0\\)\n- The discriminant is positive, confirming three distinct real roots.\n\nThus, the cubic equation is \\(\\boxed{x^3 - 5x - 2 = 0}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let the cubic equation be \\( x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), and let the roots be \\( x_1, x_2, x_3 \\). By Vieta's formulas, we have:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &a = -(x_1 + x_2 + x_3) \\\\\n   &b = x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3 \\\\\n   &c = -x_1x_2x_3 \n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n2. Given the conditions:\n   \n   - The product of the roots is 2 more than their sum:\n   \n     \\[\n     x_1x_2x_3 = x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + 2\n     \\]\n     \n   - The sum of the squares of the roots is 10:\n   \n     \\[\n     x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 = 10\n     \\]\n     \n   - The sum of the cubes of the roots is 6:\n   \n     \\[\n     x_1^3 + x_2^3 + x_3^3 = 6\n     \\]\n\n3. Using the expressions derived from Vieta's formulas and the given conditions, we first convert the conditions to use \\(a, b, c\\):\n\n   - From the sum of squares of the roots:\n   \n     \\[\n     x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 = (x_1 + x_2 + x_3)^2 - 2(x_1x_2 + x_2x_3 + x_3x_1)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = a^2 - 2b = 10\n     \\]\n     \n   - Using the sum of the cubes of the roots:\n   \n     \\[\n     x_1^3 + x_2^3 + x_3^3 = -a^3 + 3ab - 3c = 6\n     \\]\n     \n4. Rewriting the given conditions with \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   -c &= a + 2 \\\\\n   a^2 - 2b &= 10 \\\\\n   -a^3 + 3ab - 3c &= 6\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. From the first equation, solving for \\(c\\):\n\n   \\[\n   c = -a - 2\n   \\]\n\n6. From the second equation, solving for \\(b\\):\n\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{a^2}{2} - 5\n   \\]\n\n7. Substitute \\(c\\) and \\(b\\) into the third equation:\n\n   \\[\n   -a^3 + 3a\\left(\\frac{a^2}{2} - 5\\right) - 3(-a - 2) = 6\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   -a^3 + \\frac{3a^3}{2} - 15a + 3a + 6 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -a^3 + \\frac{3a^3}{2} - 12a = 0\n   \\]\n   Multiply through by 2:\n   \\[\n   -2a^3 + 3a^3 - 24a = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a^3 - 24a = 0\n   \\]\n   Factor:\n   \\[\n   a(a^2 - 24) = 0\n   \\]\n   Thus, solving for \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad a = 6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad a = -6\n   \\]\n\n8. Corresponding to these \\(a\\) values, find \\(b\\) and \\(c\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\text{For } a = 0: &\\quad b = -5, &\\quad c = -2 \\\\\n   \\text{For } a = 6: &\\quad b = 13, &\\quad c = 4 \\\\\n   \\text{For } a = -6: &\\quad b = 13, &\\quad c = -8\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n9. Form the equations:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &x^3 - 5x - 2 = 0 \\\\\n   &x^3 + 6x^2 + 13x + 4 = 0 \\\\\n   &x^3 + 6x^2 + 13x - 8 = 0\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n10. Determining the roots of each equation:\n\n    - For \\( x^3 - 5x - 2 = 0 \\):\n      \n      Calculate the discriminant \\(\\left(\\frac{-5}{3}\\right)^3 + \\left(\\frac{-2}{2}\\right)^2\\):\n      \n      \\[\n      \\left(\\frac{-5}{3}\\right)^3 + \\left(\\frac{-2}{2}\\right)^2 = -\\frac{125}{27} + 1 = -\\frac{98}{27}\n      \\]\n      Since the discriminant is negative, there are three different real roots.\n      \n    - For \\( x^3 + 6x^2 + 13x + 4 = 0 \\):\n    \n      Calculate the discriminant $\\left(\\frac{6}{3}\\right)^3 + \\left(\\frac{-4}{2}\\right)^2$:\n    \n      \\[\n      \\left(\\frac{6}{3}\\right)^3 + \\left(\\frac{-4}{2}\\right)^2 = 8 + 4 = 12\n      \\]\n      Since the discriminant is positive, there is only one real root.\n    \n    - For \\( x^3 + 6x^2 + 13x - 8 = 0 \\):\n    \n      Calculate the discriminant \\(\\left(\\frac{6}{3}\\right)^3 + \\left(\\frac{8}{2}\\right)^2\\):\n    \n      \\[\n      \\left(\\frac{6}{3}\\right)^3 + \\left(\\frac{8}{2}\\right)^2 = 8 + 16 = 24\n      \\]\n      Since the discriminant is positive, there is only one real root.\n\nThus, the sought cubic equation with three real roots is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^3 - 5x - 2 = 0}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Is it possible to arrange four identical rectangles on a plane so that no vertex is common to all rectangles, but any two rectangles have exactly one common vertex? (Rectangles can overlap each other.)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if four identical rectangles can be arranged on a plane such that no single vertex is shared by all four rectangles, but any two rectangles have exactly one common vertex. Hmm, let me start by understanding the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, the rectangles are identical, so they all have the same dimensions. They can overlap, but the 