 f(z) = 0 \\) for all \\( z \\geq 2 \\).\n\n3. **Considering \\( y < 2 \\)**:\n   - For \\( x = 2 - y \\), the equation becomes \\( f((2 - y) \\cdot f(y)) \\cdot f(y) = f(2) \\). Since \\( f(2) = 0 \\), \\( (2 - y) \\cdot f(y) \\geq 2 \\). This implies \\( f(y) \\geq \\frac{2}{2 - y} \\).\n\n4. **Verifying the function**:\n   - We hypothesize \\( f(y) = \\frac{2}{2 - y} \\) for \\( y < 2 \\). This satisfies \\( (2 - y) \\cdot f(y) = 2 \\), leading to \\( f(2) = 0 \\).\n   - Checking the functional equation for \\( x + y < 2 \\), we find both sides equal \\( \\frac{2}{2 - (x + y)} \\). For \\( x + y \\geq 2 \\), both sides are zero.\n\n5. **Uniqueness check**:\n   - Any deviation from \\( f(y) = \\frac{2}{2 - y} \\) leads to contradictions with the functional equation, confirming uniqueness.\n\nThus, the function is uniquely determined as:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(x) = \\begin{cases} \n\\dfrac{2}{2 - x} & \\text{if } 0 \\leq x < 2, \\\\ \n0 & \\text{if } x \\geq 2. \n\\end{cases}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Analyzing the functional equation and initial conditions**:\n   - We are given that \\( f(x) \\) satisfies:\n     \\[\n     f(x \\cdot f(y)) \\cdot f(y) = f(x + y) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x, y \\geq 0\n     \\]\n   - With initial conditions:\n     \\[\n     f(2) = 0\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     f(x) \\neq 0 \\quad \\text{for all} \\quad 0 \\leq x < 2\n     \\]\n   \n2. **Evaluating \\( f(x) \\) for \\( x \\geq 2 \\)**:\n   - Let \\( x > 2 \\) and \\( y = 2 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(x) = f((x-2) + 2) = f((x-2) \\cdot f(2)) \\cdot f(2)\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( f(2) = 0 \\):\n     \\[\n     f((x-2) \\cdot 0) \\cdot 0 = f(x-2 + 2) = f(x)\n     \\]\n   - This simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     f(0) \\cdot 0 = f(x)\n     \\]\n   - Irrespective of the value of \\( f(0) \\), the right-hand side is zero:\n     \\[\n     f(x) = 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x \\geq 2\n     \\]\n\n3. **Evaluating \\( f(x) \\) for \\( 0 \\leq x < 2 \\)**:\n   - Let \\( x < 2 \\). Consider \\( 2 